WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.950
To close, a rollout sequence, the
decisions to take, and the next steps.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.112
The sequence follows Book 08's
canonical order, adapted for divisions.

3
00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:15.337
One: inventory everything first,
zones, scopes, forwarders, and every

4
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:21.213
record Active Directory DNS holds,
before any domain controller is touched.

5
00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:25.424
Two: build the central pieces,
the Grid Master and candidates,

6
00:00:25.424 --> 00:00:28.793
the HSM if you'll use one,
and a build standard that

7
00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:32.499
sets the DNSSEC algorithm,
logging and STIG settings.

8
00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:36.923
Three: coexist, by managing the
existing Windows DNS and DHCP

9
00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:41.700
servers from the Grid in place, so
nothing moves until you're ready.

10
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.771
Four: onboard divisions one at a
time, starting with pattern (a),

11
00:00:45.771 --> 00:00:51.063
the simplest, and adding views or
delegations only where a division needs them.

12
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:53.299
Five: federate the clouds.

13
00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:57.730
Six: harden and evidence, with the
STIG applied, logs at retention,

14
00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.325
and audit trails the
authorizing official can read.

15
00:01:01.325 --> 00:01:03.000
The decisions to take now.

16
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.188
Which platform model, NIOS, Universal
DDI or hybrid, for each impact level.

17
00:01:09.188 --> 00:01:11.702
Which delegation pattern
for each division,

18
00:01:11.702 --> 00:01:15.438
and who approves changes for a
bureau that needs confidentiality.

19
00:01:15.438 --> 00:01:21.188
Whether to wait for the FIPS 140-3
certificate or proceed with a POA&M.

20
00:01:21.188 --> 00:01:24.488
And where logs will live to
stay searchable for six months.

21
00:01:24.488 --> 00:01:25.737
And the next steps.

22
00:01:25.737 --> 00:01:29.150
Send Infoblox the procurement
asks from Module 2.

23
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.619
Confirm which roles access views support,

24
00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:34.300
and what value Entra
sends for group matching.

25
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.149
Lab-test encrypted forwarding to CISA's

26
00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:40.500
Protective DNS and the
DNSSEC algorithm change.

27
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.763
And draft each division's scope and the

28
00:01:42.763 --> 00:01:45.662
responsibility matrix
with the division itself.

29
00:01:45.662 --> 00:01:51.043
In the program's analysis, Infoblox is one
of the strongest ways to build a governed

30
00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:55.712
DDI plane for a federal agency, as long
as you manage its risks in the open.

31
00:01:55.712 --> 00:01:59.858
One note on standing: this is
the program's research analysis,

32
00:01:59.858 --> 00:02:03.588
under review, not an endorsement
by any federal agency.

33
00:02:03.588 --> 00:02:04.350
Thank you.

34
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:08.998
Sources: Book 08, index (canonical
sequence) and Chapter 10;

35
00:02:08.998 --> 00:02:12.953
Infoblox NIOS and Universal
DDI documentation;

36
00:02:12.953 --> 00:02:18.840
federal requirements and validation
research cited in Modules 1 and 2.
